{
 "id": "retrieval-core",
 "version": "v1",
 "about": "One fixed world state (60 facts about one owner) plus 200 queries with graded relevance judgments. Measures whether AQUA can FIND what it stored, including the adversarial cases this codebase has actually shipped failures on.",
 "limitations": [
  "SYNTHETIC. The corpus and queries were authored together, so a query is more likely to have a findable answer than in real use. A system tuned against this can look better than it is.",
  "One annotator. Relevance judgments carry no measured inter-annotator agreement, and relevance is the most subjective part of any IR benchmark.",
  "60 facts is a SMALL corpus. Lexical precision degrades with corpus size, so scores here are an upper bound on what the same engine would do at 5,000 facts. RETRIEVE-SCALE (Blueprint Part 11) is the missing companion.",
  "Two relevance grades only (relevant=2, acceptable=1). Finer grades would be invented precision with a single annotator.",
  "English only, single-turn. No conversational follow-up, so pronoun resolution across turns is unmeasurable here.",
  "Why-questions are not represented. Causal retrieval has nothing to score against yet: the engine's chain builder deliberately names its output `progression` rather than causation, because it cannot detect cause. When that changes, this dataset needs a `reason` category."
 ],
